Self-adaptive temperature and humidity compensation based on improved deep BP neural network for NO2 detection in complex environment
Zhen Wang a,1, Chunyan Xie b,1, Bohao Liu b, Yadong Jiang b, Zhemin Li a,c, Huiling Tai b,*,
Xian Li a,*
a Agricultural Information Institute, Chinese Academy of Agricultural Sciences, Beijing 100081, China
b State Key Laboratory of Electronic Thin Films and Integrated Devices, School of Optoelectronic Science and Engineering, University of Electronic Science and Technology of China, Chengdu 610054, China
c Graduate School of Chinese Academy of Agricultural Sciences, Beijing 100081, China
ARTICLE INFO
Keywords: Gas sensor Temperature and humidity compensation Improved deep BP neural network Self-learning Self-adaptability Recognition accuracy
ABSTRACT
The accuracy and reliability of gas sensor are directly by temperature and humidity. In this study, an improved deep Back Propagation (BP) neural network was designed to lower the impact of environmental factors on NO2 gas sensor based on PbS nanoparticles sensitive film. The contradiction between high performance and low time complexity was usually faced by current compensation methods of gas sensor. Moreover, poor self-learning ability always resulted in more recognition errors. To solve the above problems, a 14-layer deep BP neural network model was constructed after hyperparameter searching. Stochastic Gradient Descent (SGD) algorithm with Mini-batch algorithm was adopted to well balance the model performance and the training time complexity, resulting in 76.68% performance improvement and nearly 6 times training time reduction after 1000 epochs, respectively. Softplus activation function was combined with Adam optimizer to further improve the model performance with a good recognition accuracy (1.37% relative error, corresponding to 0.0087 Mean Square Error (MSE)). The self-learning and self-adaptability of the improved deep BP neural network made it an excellent compensation method for the gas sensor applied in complex environments.
1. Introduction
During the process of human production and life, a large amount of NO2 emissions have caused serious nitrogen pollution problems, such as eutrophication of surface waters, accumulation of underground nitrates, and decline in biodiversity. Nitrogen pollution makes the ecological environment problems more prominent, and brings a certain threat to human health [1]. Therefore, the accuracy of NO2 detection is of great significance for monitoring and reducing nitrogen pollution [2]. However, in complex environment, the constant changes of temperature and humidity bring huge impacts on the accuracy of NO2 sensor. Establishing a temperature and humidity compensation model is an effective strategy to lower the influence of temperature and humidity and realize the accurate monitoring of gas sensor. At present, the compensation method of gas sensor has been widely adopted, such as linear regression fitting, nonlinear regression fitting, machine learning algorithm these software algorithm compensations,
and some other hardware compensations. Linear regression fitting commonly constructed a linear model with ambient temperature and sensor response as regression variables [3,4]. When encountering non-linear data, it was prone to under fitting of data with low prediction accuracy, so it is not suitable for compensation under the joint action of temperature and humidity. Non-linear regression fitting can improve the measurement accuracy of the inductive proximity sensor and reduce the distance deviation of the ASIC chip, thereby improving the accuracy of the measurement [5]. Machine learning algorithms, such as random forest algorithm, compensated the temperature and humidity of metal oxide semiconductor (MOS) gas sensor [6]. However, the generalization capability of nonlinear regression fitting and machine learning algorithms was poor, as well as the weak learning capability, resulting in large test error [7,8]. The software compensation methods have certain applicability to sensors with different detection targets in a specific environment. In contrast, hardware compensation methods completely depend on the performance of the sensor hardware itself, leading to poor
