 used to construct the hidden layer, while its performance in nonlinear regression tasks was not satisfying and resulted in unreliable compensation effects. Therefore, the Mean Square Error (MSE) of BP neural network can be further reduced through optimizing the above issues. In this study, NO2 gas sensor based PbS sensitive film with big and fast response was fabricated as a representative gas sensor, and the resistance response to NO2 gas at different temperature and humidity conditions were obtained. An improved deep BP neural network with 14-layer was constructed in Pytorch framework. Stochastic Gradient Descent (SGD) algorithm with Mini-batch was carried out to balance the model performance and time complexity. The activation function and optimizer were studied and optimized to improve the recognition accuracy of the model. The self-adaptation and self-learning capabilities of the improved model were analyzed and verified as well.
2. Methodology
2.1. NO2 sensor fabrication
NO2 sensor based PbS sensitive film was fabricated. PbS nanoparticles were fabricated via a simple coprecipitation method: 0.331 g of lead nitrate (Pb(NO3)2, Chengdu Jinshan Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd) and 0.24 g of sodium sulfide nonahydrate (Na2S·9H2O, Kelong Chemical Reagent Company of Chengdu) were dissolved in a mixed solution containing 20 mL of ethanol and 20 mL of deionized water under vigorous stirring for 30 min. After that, the black precipitates were centrifuged at 3000 rpm for 3 min, then washed with ethanol and deionized water alternately for three times, and dried at 60 ◦C in an oven for 6 h before use. All the materials used in the experiment were analytical grade. The entire synthesis process was simple, controllable and environmental-friendly with low cost. Based on our previous work [16], the flexible polyimide (PI) substrate (thickness: ~123 μm, Egene Optoelectronic Materials Co., Ltd) with gold interdigital electrodes (IDEs) were fabricated and hydrophilicity-treated before use. 10 mg/mL of PbS-water dispersion was prepared, ultrasonic-treated, and then spin-coated onto the IDEs for
27 times. Finally, the as-prepared device was dried at 60 ◦C in an oven.
2.2. Measurements and data acquisition
The gas sensing properties of the fabricated PbS based NO2 sensor were measured by a homemade dynamic test system as previous [17]. The NO2 standard gases (100 ppm, air as balance gas) and carrier gas (dry air) were supplied by Chengdu Xuyuan Chemical Co., Ltd. The sensor was placed in a chamber with gas inlet and outlet, which is exposed to NO2 gas. The concentration of NO2 gas was controlled by diluting the supplied NO2 standard gas with dry air using a mass flow controller. The bubbling method was used with mass flow controller to supply different humidity test conditions, where the relative humidity values were measured. The chamber was placed into a temperature control box calibrated by a high-accuracy thermometer (HC2-S, Rotronic Co. Ltd.), keeping the entire temperature of temperature control box became stable for 30 min before starting gas/humidity distribution system. A temperature and humidity sensor (SHT31-ARP, Sensirion, Switzerland) was placed in the chamber to calibrate the accuracy of the test environment parameters. The resistances of the sensors were recorded by a real-time multimeter resistance acquisition system (Keithley 2700), as shown as Fig. S1. The real-time response/recovery performance of this NO2 sensor to 2–10 ppm (2 ppm as a step) under different environmental conditions was obtained. The humidity was ranged from 20% to 90% RH, with 10% RH as a step. All the humidity values were set and measured under the temperature values of 10, 20, 30, 40 and 50 ◦C, respectively. The range of temperature was 10–50 ◦C, with 10 ◦C as a step, referring to the range of temperature variation in the agricultural greenhouse environment (Fig. S2). The resistance value at the maximum resistance change of the sensor was extracted, totaling 240 samples. Moreover, in order to prove the generalization ability of the model, commercial NO2 sensor (CLE
0321–400, Honeywell) was also adopted and tested with 5 ◦C as the temperature step and the humidity continuously changed in the range of 11–80%. The maximum voltage value of the commercial NO2 sensor during the test process was extracted, as the same as maximum resistance value of the fabricated PbS based NO2 sensor, totaling 51,375 samples.
