3. Results and discussions
3.1. The resistance response of NO2 sensor and data standardization
The resistance response performances of PbS based NO2 sensor under different temperature and humidity conditions were obtained, and the resistance value at the maximum resistance change sensor was extracted, totaling 240 samples (Fig. 2). The fabricated sensor exhibited high response and fast speed to NO2, which can fully meet the needs of agricultural gas detection. PbS is a typical p-type semiconductor material. The oxygen molecules adsorbed on the surface of PbS cause electrons transfer, and further interacts with NO2 molecule, finally resulting in an obvious decrease in resistance of PbS [25–27]. Fig. 2a–e depict the resistance response changes of NO2 sensor with the increasing concentration of NO2 sensor at 20–90% RH under different temperature, respectively. It could be seen that the resistance approximately linear growth with the increasing humidity, when exposed in dry air, excepting at the temperature of 30 and 50 ◦C. At 30 ◦C condition, the resistance at 90% RH decreased a little compared with 80% RH. While at 50 ◦C, the resistance expressed irregular changes. Totally, with the increasing concentration of NO2, the resistance of the sensor changes more widely. However, the magnitudes of the resistance change were irregular. Fig. 2f showed the influence of temperature on the resistance response to 2 ppm NO2 at 20–90% RH. It seemed that temperature had a more dramatic effect on sensor performance compared to humidity, and showed a non-linear change tendency. For example, at 50 ◦C condition, the resistance response at 70% and 50% RH were higher than that at 80% RH, indicating the complexity of the environment’s impact on sensor performances. The commercial NO2 sensor had the similar phenomenon, especially at high humidity condition (Fig. S3). Therefore, the temperature and humidity compensation algorithm should have self-learning and adaptive capabilities, thus to guarantee the accuracy of sensor response performances under the influence of complex environments. Data standardization was carried out firstly as Eq. (1). After standardization, the mean values of temperature, humidity and sensor resistance response were 0, and the standard deviations were 1. A batch with a capacity of 16 was randomly selected and shown in Table 1. After that, 225 samples were randomly selected as the training set, accounting for 93.75% of the total data set. The rest 15 samples were the development set automatically.
